---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/logistics-supply-chain/foundational/supply-chain-concepts-and-metrics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Supply chain management is the coordination of sourcing, production and delivery across
    companies that don't share a boss. Its lens is the flow: product downstream, money upstream,
    information both ways. Managed well, the chain is invisible; managed badly, it is empty shelves,
    idled factories and a very loud news cycle.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="flows, trade-offs and the SCOR vocabulary" widget="equationStep" config={chainCfg} />

  <h2>The bullwhip: uncertainty multiplies upstream</h2>
  <p>
    Retailers pad orders against uncertainty; wholesalers pad against retailers' padding; factories
    see the compounded wave. The result — demand variability growing stage by stage — is the
    bullwhip effect, the reason a supermarket hiccup becomes a factory's boom-and-bust. Fixes are
    informational: share point-of-sale data, cut batch sizes, stabilise prices.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="each stage orders more wildly than the last" widget="plotter" config={bullwhipCfg} />

  <h2>Metrics make the chain discussable</h2>
  <p>
    Perfect order rate, forecast accuracy, inventory turns, cash-to-cash cycle time: the CSCP
    syllabus's metrics exist because chains span companies, and only agreed numbers can be managed
    across a boundary. Inventory turns, for instance, compresses the whole inventory-vs-service
    tension into one comparable figure.
  </p>

  <h2>Strategy: responsive or efficient</h2>
  <p>
    Every chain makes a fundamental positioning choice: efficient (predictable products, cost
    focus, slow pipelines — think detergent) or responsive (volatile demand, speed focus — think
    fashion). Fisher's classic insight is that matching chain type to product type is the strategy;
    mismatches are where supply chains visibly fail.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> ASCM CSCP Exam Content Manual; Simchi-Levi et al., <em>Designing and Managing the Supply Chain</em>; Fisher, "What Is the Right Supply Chain for Your Product?" (1997).</p>
</TopicPage>
